  "body": "## Request\n\nAdd a post for classic Grbl 1.1 (3-axis mill). Many small routers and hobby mills run Grbl 1.1 on an Arduino-class board. The `grblHAL` post (#710) writes words that Grbl 1.1 refuses, so it is not a replacement.\n\n## Differences to the grblHAL post\n\nFrom the public Grbl 1.1 documentation (the \"Commands\" and \"Interface\" wiki pages, behaviour only):\n\n- No canned cycles. Grbl 1.1 has `G80` but not `G73`, `G81`-`G89`. The post must expand drill, peck and bore cycles to `G0` / `G1` / `G4` moves.\n- No `M6`. A tool change is `M5`, a retract to a safe Z in machine coordinates (`G53 G0 Z...`), then `M0` with a comment that names the tool. `T` is written in a comment only.\n- No `G43 H`. The length offset is `G43.1 Z...` or nothing. Default: write no length offset (the user zeroes each tool), and write `G49` in the preamble.\n- No `O` program number. No `G41` / `G42`. No `G98` / `G99`.\n- `G4 P` is in seconds.\n- Line buffer of 80 characters: keep each block short. Comments are `( )` only. Do not write comments with nested brackets.\n- Coolant: `M7`, `M8`, `M9` only. Other coolant kinds are refused by name.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A built-in post `Grbl 1.1` and a machine file `grbl-3axis.mch`.\n- Goldens for contour, pocket, drill (expanded), peck (expanded), bore with dwell, two tools.\n- A test that each posted line is 80 characters or less, and that no unsupported word (`G8x`, `M6`, `G43 H`, `G41`, `G42`, `O`) is posted.\n\nI can do this work in a PR from my fork.\n",
